---
// The Buzzit case study in the two-sidebar layout (src/components/CaseLayout.astro), 2026-11-26.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Foot from '../../components/Foot.astro';
import CaseLayout from '../../components/CaseLayout.astro';
import oldFeed from '../../assets/buzzit/old-feed.png';
import oldSearch from '../../assets/buzzit/old-search.png';
import oldBooking from '../../assets/buzzit/old-booking.png';
import home from '../../assets/buzzit/home-clean.png';
import listing from '../../assets/buzzit/listing-request-clean.png';
import exploreSocial from '../../assets/buzzit/explore-social.png';
import exploreMarket from '../../assets/buzzit/explore-marketplace.png';
import exploreHybrid from '../../assets/buzzit/explore-hybrid.png';
import cutRankings from '../../assets/buzzit/cut-rankings.png';
import cutLearn from '../../assets/buzzit/cut-learn.png';
import cutHives from '../../assets/buzzit/cut-private-hives.png';
import v1Discover from '../../assets/buzzit/v1-discover.png';
import v1Social from '../../assets/buzzit/v1-social.png';
import v1Post from '../../assets/buzzit/cut-post-types.png';
import req1 from '../../assets/buzzit/requests-v1.png';
import req2 from '../../assets/buzzit/requests-v2.png';
import req3 from '../../assets/buzzit/requests-v3.png';
import tabSocial from '../../assets/buzzit/tabbar-social.png';
import tabAsks from '../../assets/buzzit/tabbar-asks.png';
import tabFinal from '../../assets/buzzit/tabbar-final.png';
import list1 from '../../assets/buzzit/listing-v1.png';
import list2 from '../../assets/buzzit/listing-v2.png';
import list3 from '../../assets/buzzit/listing-v3.png';
import spec from '../../assets/buzzit/spec-order-handoff.png';
import team1 from '../../assets/buzzit/team-1.png';
import team2 from '../../assets/buzzit/team-2.png';

const ph = { widths: [300, 500, 800], sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 240px, 25vw', quality: 88 };
const tabs: [string, string][] = [['overview', 'Overview'], ['problem', 'Problem'], ['solution', 'Solution'], ['explore', 'Explore'], ['v1', 'Version 1'], ['decisions', 'Design decisions'], ['shipping', 'Shipping'], ['results', 'Results'], ['takeaways', 'Takeaways']];
const facts: [string, string][] = [['Role', 'Sole product designer'], ['Timeline', 'Summer 2026'], ['Team', '2 co-founders, 2 developers, 1 marketer']];
---
<Base title="Buzzit: a student marketplace, a case study by Ian Kim" description="Students wanted Buzzit, but only 22% of bookings finished. I redesigned the app so discovery leads to bookings and bookings get finished.">
  <div data-light="quiet">
  <CaseLayout label="Buzzit" title="From “I want this” to “we met up”" tabs={tabs} facts={facts} next={{ href: '/work/moober', title: 'Moober' }}>
    <figure slot="open" class="cd__fig">
      <div class="cd__plate">
        <div class="cd__phones cd__phones--two">
          <Image src={home} alt="The new Home: search, categories, a featured maker and listings with prices." {...ph} />
          <Image src={listing} alt="A gel manicure listing with the seller, Message, the seller's question and Request to book." {...ph} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <section class="cd__sec">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Overview</p>
        <h2>A marketplace made for UC Davis students</h2>
        <p>Buzzit is where UC Davis students sell services and things to each other: nails, cakes, tutoring, grad photos, dorm furniture. Fiverr and TaskRabbit feel built for professionals, and Instagram groups aren't built for booking, so Buzzit puts visual discovery, .edu-verified trust and booking in one student-only app. I was the only designer on the summer redesign, which relaunched on October 1.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="problem">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Problem</p>
        <h2>Demand wasn't the problem. The path to meeting up was.</h2>
        <p>The spring launch showed students wanted Buzzit, but very few of the things they wanted ever turned into a finished meet-up. Every time change started a back-and-forth that busy students gave up on, and the Pinterest-style feed was fun, but students didn't use it to find things.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cd__stats">
        <div class="cd__stat"><span class="cd__num">22%</span><p>of bookings were completed: 63 of 283</p></div>
        <div class="cd__stat"><span class="cd__num">0</span><p>bookings from 10,000 feed impressions, at a 2.3% tap rate</p></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="cd__fig">
        <div class="cd__plate">
          <div class="cd__phones">
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={oldFeed} alt="The spring app: a Pinterest-style feed of photos." {...ph} /><span>Feed</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={oldSearch} alt="The spring app's Businesses tab: a search bar over a grid of sellers." {...ph} /><span>Businesses</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={oldBooking} alt="Booking a service by picking a time slot." {...ph} /><span>Booking</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>The spring app</span><span>Spring 2026, 940 users</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p>That set three goals for the summer: make discovery lead to bookings, make bookings easy to finish, and build a student community.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="solution">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Solution</p>
        <h2>A marketplace students already know how to use</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cd__pair">
        <div><h4>Decide from the feed</h4><p>Price on every card and broad categories, so a student can decide from the feed instead of tapping into every listing.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Agree in a chat</h4><p>A request that opens a chat instead of a calendar, so the time gets agreed the way students already do it.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="explore">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Explore</p>
        <h2>Four directions in a week, then a hybrid</h2>
        <p>The team explored four very different directions: social communities called Hives, a camera-first app with points (Justin), streaks and rankings (Vedant), and a marketplace (Shota and me). Each had something worth keeping, so Venice and I combined them into a hybrid and each interviewed three students with it.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="cd__fig">
        <div class="cd__plate">
          <div class="cd__phones">
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={exploreSocial} alt="A teammate's Hives mockup: communities like Nail Techs and Barbers Guild, with Join buttons." {...ph} /><span>Social</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={exploreMarket} alt="The marketplace direction: moving-out deals and services with prices and ratings." {...ph} /><span>Marketplace</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={exploreHybrid} alt="The hybrid Discover screen with a featured maker and popular listings." {...ph} /><span>Hybrid</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>Three of the directions</span><span>The Social mockup was a teammate's quick sketch, and still says Stanford</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="cd__sub">
        <div class="cd__text">
          <h3>What six students told us</h3>
          <p>Paraphrased from the hybrid interviews. The marketplace layout tested best, so it became the core, with community ideas built around it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cd__pair cd__pair--quiet cd__pair--four">
          <div><p>Prices and ratings up front make it easy to decide.</p></div>
          <div><p>The old feed feels overwhelming.</p></div>
          <div><p>Private groups belong in our group chats. Clubs could use Hives, though.</p></div>
          <div><p>Streaks mean nothing without a reward, and the reward we'd want is discounts.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cd__sub">
        <div class="cd__text">
          <h3>Cut before building</h3>
          <p>Every feature had to answer one question: does this help a student get from &ldquo;I want this&rdquo; to &ldquo;we met up&rdquo;? Streaks went, since discounts would be expensive and work against affordable prices. The Learn tab went, since students already learn on TikTok. Private Hives became public, club-focused ones.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="cd__fig">
          <div class="cd__plate">
            <div class="cd__phones">
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={cutRankings} alt="UC Davis rankings: a leaderboard of students by streak." {...ph} /><span>Streaks and rankings</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={cutLearn} alt="A Learn tab inside a Nails at UC Davis hive." {...ph} /><span>Learn tab</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={cutHives} alt="Explore hives, with private communities joined by invite link." {...ph} /><span>Private Hives</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>Cut before v1</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="v1">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Version 1</p>
        <h2>The concepts weren't wrong. There were just too many of them.</h2>
        <p>I presented v1 on July 15, then led the next round of research: I split the interviews across all seven teammates, five each, and went through all <strong>35 interviews</strong> myself to find the main problems.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="cd__fig">
        <div class="cd__plate">
          <div class="cd__phones">
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={v1Discover} alt="Version 1 Discover: search, categories, a featured maker and listings." {...ph} /><span>Discover</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={v1Social} alt="Version 1 Social: a row of open asks above posts from hives." {...ph} /><span>Social</span></div>
            <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={v1Post} alt="Version 1 New post: four post types (Showcase, Progress, Question, Model call), posted to a hive." {...ph} /><span>New post</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>Version 1</span><span>Presented July 15</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="cd__pair">
        <div><h4>What students liked</h4><ul><li>Discover made browsing easy</li><li>Profiles felt clear and personal</li><li>Onboarding was smooth</li></ul></div>
        <div><h4>Where students got lost</h4><ul><li>Hives only made sense after reading the onboarding</li><li>Open asks looked like Hive posts</li><li>Three different places to start a post</li><li>Notifications, reviews and messages were easy to miss</li></ul></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="decisions">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Design decisions</p>
        <h2>Simplifying, version by version</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="cd__sub" id="requests">
        <div class="cd__text">
          <p class="label">Decision 01</p>
          <h3>Requests: from a tab to a list on Home</h3>
          <p>In v1, open asks sat in a small carousel next to Hive posts, and testers couldn't tell them apart. In v2 they got their own Asks tab, with filters and an &ldquo;I need this too&rdquo; button. Then I challenged my own design: a new feature with its own tab can easily look empty. I searched Reddit for &ldquo;looking for&rdquo; posts from Davis students and found about 20 to 30 over two to three quarters. Real demand, but not enough to fill a tab. In v3, Requests became a simple list on Home, with replies right on the card.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="cd__fig">
          <div class="cd__plate">
            <div class="cd__phones">
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={req1} alt="Version 1: open asks in a carousel inside the Social tab." {...ph} /><span>V1</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={req2} alt="Version 2: an Asks tab with filters and Wondering too buttons." {...ph} /><span>V2</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={req3} alt="Version 3: Requests as a list on Home, with I need this too and reply counts." {...ph} /><span>V3, shipped</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <div class="cd__text">
          <p>The second tab went from Hives to Asks to Saved. Hives was cut from launch for the same reason: social features only work when lots of people use them.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="cd__fig">
          <div class="cd__plate">
            <div class="cd__tabbars">
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={tabSocial} alt="The v1 tab bar with a Social tab." widths={[400, 786]} sizes="420px" /><span>V1</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={tabAsks} alt="The v2 tab bar with an Asks tab." widths={[400, 786]} sizes="420px" /><span>V2</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={tabFinal} alt="The shipped tab bar: Home, Saved, create, Inbox and You." widths={[400, 786]} sizes="420px" /><span>Shipped</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p class="cd__line">An empty Saved tab reads as the student's own doing. An empty Asks tab reads as a verdict on the app.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cd__sub" id="listing">
        <div class="cd__text">
          <p class="label">Decision 02</p>
          <h3>Listing questions: the builder was deleted</h3>
          <p>Sellers often need something up front: a nail tech needs the length, a baker needs the name for the cake. V1 had builders for options and paid extras, plus a box explaining the difference, which was a sign the screen wasn't explaining itself. V2 moved that meaning into the labels, but sellers were still lost. Since Buzzit takes no payment, those totals were math nobody would be charged for. V3 deleted the builder: one optional field, &ldquo;Ask the buyer something&rdquo;, in the seller's own words.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="cd__fig">
          <div class="cd__plate">
            <div class="cd__phones">
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={list1} alt="Version 1: price and options, with builders for options and extras and a rule-of-thumb box." {...ph} /><span>V1</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={list2} alt="Version 2: the same builders with the explanation written into the labels." {...ph} /><span>V2</span></div>
              <div class="cd__ph"><Image src={list3} alt="Version 3: the review step with one optional field, Ask the buyer something." {...ph} /><span>V3, shipped</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="cd__sub" id="booking">
        <div class="cd__text">
          <p class="label">Decision 03</p>
          <h3>Booking by chat</h3>
          <p>Texting is how students already coordinate everything. Now a request opens a chat, the two agree on a time and place, and one tap confirms it. Some students asked for a calendar, but a calendar only works if sellers keep it current, and in spring they often didn't. The time and place are still recorded on the order.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="cd__fig">
          <div class="cd__plate">
            <div class="cd__flow">
              <p class="label">Before</p>
              <ol><li>Pick a time</li><li>Send request</li><li>Seller accepts, reschedules or declines</li><li>Start again</li></ol>
              <p class="label">After</p>
              <ol class="is-after"><li>Tap request</li><li>Chat opens</li><li>Agree on the details</li><li>Confirm in one tap</li></ol>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="shipping">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Shipping</p>
        <h2>Every state written down, so developers weren't guessing</h2>
        <p>I wrote lifecycle specs for every major flow, covering each state and edge case: a one-of-a-kind jacket can't be reserved twice, orders keep what was agreed even if the listing changes, and orders nobody confirms expire. Cutting Hives and the listing builder took two of the biggest pieces of work off a small team.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="cd__fig">
        <div class="cd__plate"><Image class="cd__wide" src={spec} alt="The Order and Handoff lifecycle spec: four states, one door in." widths={[800, 1200, 1385]} sizes="760px" /></div>
        <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>Order and handoff</span><span>One of the lifecycle specs</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="results">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Results</p>
        <h2>Completed bookings went from about 22% to about 92%</h2>
        <p>The fall numbers are a month in and the user base is smaller, and almost everything changed at once, so no single change gets the credit. Growing the number of users is the next challenge.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cd__stats">
        <div class="cd__stat"><span class="cd__num">22%</span><p>Spring launch: 63 of 283 bookings completed, from 940 users</p></div>
        <div class="cd__stat"><span class="cd__num">92%</span><p>Since October 1: 56 of 61 completed, from 147 users in the first month</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cd__sec" id="takeaways">
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p class="label">Takeaways</p>
        <h2>What I took away</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="cd__list cd__text">
        <li><div><h4>Cut before adding</h4><p>Fewer features made Buzzit easier to use.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h4>Design for the supply you have</h4><p>Empty screens make a new app look dead.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h4>Challenge your own decisions with evidence</h4><p>The Requests tab was my idea, and my own Reddit research showed it wasn't ready.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="cd__text">
        <p>Next, Hives returns when there are enough students to fill it, and we keep interviewing students to find where Buzzit still falls short.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="cd__fig">
        <div class="cd__photos">
          <Image src={team1} alt="Part of the Buzzit team at an event, smiling with lanyards on." widths={[500, 800]} sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 340px, 100vw" />
          <Image src={team2} alt="The Buzzit team standing together in matching navy shirts." widths={[600, 1080]} sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 460px, 100vw" />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cd__cap"><span>The Buzzit team</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </CaseLayout>
  </div>
  <Foot />
</Base>
